      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town once thrived as a port, with steamboats serving Vaughn and neighboring communities from the 1870s to the 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaughn%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1894, the community built a library hall by converting a dance floor, later becoming a private residence after the library moved in 1957.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.keypeninsulamuseum.org/key-communities/vaughn-bay/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Key Peninsula Historical Society now owns and restores the historic Vaughn Hall, preserving the town’s maritime and farming heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.keypeninsulamuseum.org/key-communities/vaughn-bay/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 50‑mile historical route curated by the Key Peninsula Historical Society links almost 120 points of interest, including Vaughn, across the peninsula.",
